  "conversion_warnings": [
    "Skeletal source file was largely incorrect and was discarded in favor of the live NMSU catalog (catalogs.nmsu.edu, CourseLeaf). The source mis-categorized every block as 'elective', omitted all General Education / Viewing-a-Wider-World / non-departmental supporting requirements, dropped the gen-ed math (MATH 1511G/1521G), used the wrong degree id ('...-elective'), and did not include the larger general-CS elective pool. Rebuilt with proper major/supporting/gen_ed/elective categories.",
    "NEAR-DUPLICATE SIBLING NOTE: NMSU lists this general 'Computer Science - Bachelor of Science' alongside a separate 'Computer Science (Artificial Intelligence) - Bachelor of Science' concentration (and a standalone 'Artificial Intelligence - B.S.' and a 'Computer Science - B.A.'). This file is the GENERAL CS BS (no concentration): it has NO AI-concentration blocks, CSCI 3720 is part of the required core (not an algorithms choice), and the Computer Science Elective pool is the full 25-course list (adds CSCI 4235/4240/4435/4440/5310 vs. the AI sibling). Free electives are 11 here vs. 2 in the AI sibling because the AI concentration consumes 12 credits.",
    "Course credits/titles/prereqs verified against the NMSU course bubble (ribbit getcourse) pages: e.g. CSCI 3720 = 4cr (prereq CSCI 2220 & CSCI 2310); CSCI 4980 Senior Project = 4cr (prereq CSCI 3730 & CSCI 3710); CSCI 4140 = 3cr; CSCI 4235/4240/4440/5310 = 3cr (no prereqs); CSCI 4435 = 3cr (prereq CSCI 2220 & CSCI 2310). STEM lab sciences are a 3cr lecture + 1cr lab (BIOL 2610G+L, BIOL 2110G+L, PHYS 1230/1240/1310/1320 G+L, PHYS 2110/2140 +L), while CHEM 1215G/1225G, ASTR 1115G, GEOG/GEOL 1110G, HNRS 2116G are single combined 4cr lecture+lab courses.",
    "cip_code 11.0701 (Computer Science) inferred from the program being the general single-major CS degree; not stated in the catalog. (The AI-concentration sibling was tagged 11.0101; this general degree is the canonical CS program.)",
    "catalog_year set to 2026-2027 (the live NMSU catalog edition shown on the page header); not printed in the program body.",
    "Senior Project OR Senior Thesis modeled as a choice '{CSCI4980, CSCI4999}' inside the required core (catalog: 'CSCI 4980 Senior Project or CSCI 4999 Senior Thesis'). Honors equivalents (ENGL 2210H, MATH 1511H/1521H, COMM honors) are omitted as interchangeable honors sections of the listed G courses.",
    "Prereqs with grade thresholds (catalog states 'at least a C-') are modeled as plain course references; the C- requirement is captured at the degree level via grade_minimum='C-'. Prereqs simplified to in-scope courses where the catalog lists non-CS engineering/placement alternatives or out-of-scope math: CSCI 1720 modeled as requiring CSCI 1110 (catalog also allows MATH 1220+ or placement); CSCI 4205 dropped 'or consent'; MATH 4230/3110/3140 modeled on MATH 2415 (catalog also lists E E 200 / M E 228 / MATH 1531). CSCI 1110/1220 defined as supporting prereq roots, not degree requirements.",
    "CSCI 4996 Special Topics is 1-12 variable credit; per catalog footnote 6 it must be taken for 3 credits to count as a course, so it is defined at 3.0 credits as an elective option.",
    "Overlap not fully modeled (catalog footnote 5: a course can satisfy only one requirement). The lab science offerings appear in both the Gen-Ed Area III pool and the Non-Departmental Lab Science pool; constraints.exclude_used:true is set on the CS elective pool so the analyzer does not reuse a core course. allow_double_counting=false at the degree level.",
    "Credit reconciliation to total_credits=120: Gen-Ed = 41 (Area I 10 [Comp1 4 + Comp2 3 + Oral 3] + Area II 4 + Area III 8 [two 4cr lab sciences] + Area IV 3 + Area V 3 + Area VI 3 + Gen-Ed-elective/Calc II 4 + VWW 6); Department core 46 (1720/2210/2220/2230/2310/3730/3710/3720 @4 = 32, 4110 @1, senior project/thesis @4, 4105/4120/4140 @3 = 9); CS elective 6; Non-departmental 13 (linear algebra 3 + math upper elective 3 + statistics 3 + lab science 4); free electives 14 = 120 exactly, matching the catalog's stated free-elective figure (footnote 7). Area IV/V/VI, VWW, and free electives are modeled as external_requirement '*:*' wildcard pools (open NMSU distribution lists not enumerated)."
